# LWS operations -> HTTP-in-RDF request mapping rules
#
# Test steps are written as higher-level LWS operations
# (lwst:CreateResource, lwst:CreateContainer, lwst:CreateDataResource,
# lwst:ReadContainer, lwst:ReadStorageDescription, lwst:ReadResource,
# lwst:UpdateResource, lwst:DeleteResource -- cf. the operations in
# https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#operations).
# Each rule maps one operation to a basic HTTP request, described with
# the W3C HTTP Vocabulary in RDF (https://www.w3.org/TR/HTTP-in-RDF10/):
# the operation node doubles as an http:Request carrying its resolved
# http:absoluteURI and an http:mthd method. The operation's lwst:target
# is a parameter IRI; its value is bound with lwst:value, and the rule
# resolves the two into the request's http:absoluteURI. Expected
# response values are asserted per test; the rules only expose the
# generic parts of an actual http:Response under the suite's well-known
# parameter names (param:status-code, param:location, param:content --
# see the last section).
#
# The rules deliberately use only Basic Graph Patterns so they run on
# the n3.js reasoner (https://www.npmjs.com/package/n3#Reasoning).
# Conclusion blank nodes are created once per rule, not per firing --
# so per-step request data (method, URI, ...) attaches to the operation
# node itself: the operation doubles as the HTTP-in-RDF request
# description.

@prefix http:     <http://www.w3.org/2011/http#> .
@prefix methods:  <http://www.w3.org/2011/http-methods#> .
@prefix lwst:     <https://www.w3.org/ns/lws-tests/v1#> .
@prefix param:    <https://w3id.org/lws/test/param#> .
@prefix cnt:      <http://www.w3.org/2011/content#> .
@prefix lws:      <https://www.w3.org/ns/lws#> .
@prefix did:      <https://www.w3.org/ns/did#> .

# Read container -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#read-resource
# GET the container listing, negotiating the LWS container media type.
# The Accept header is constant per operation, so it can safely live on
# the (per-rule, shared) conclusion blank node.
{
  ?op a lwst:ReadContainer ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:GET ;
      http:headers [
        a http:RequestHeader ;
        http:fieldName "Accept" ;
        http:fieldValue "application/lws+json"
      ] .
} .

# Read storage description -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#discovery
# GET the storage description, negotiating the LWS CID media type.
# The Accept header is constant per operation, so it can safely live on
# the (per-rule, shared) conclusion blank node.
{
  ?op a lwst:ReadStorageDescription ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:GET ;
      http:headers [
        a http:RequestHeader ;
        http:fieldName "Accept" ;
        http:fieldValue "application/lws+cid"
      ] .
} .

# Read resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#read-resource
{
  ?op a lwst:ReadResource ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:GET .
} .

# Create resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#create-resource
# Generic create binding: POST to the target container. Use the more
# specific CreateContainer / CreateDataResource when the kind of the new
# resource is known.
{
  ?op a lwst:CreateResource ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:POST .
} .

# Create container -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#create-resource
# POST to the target container with a Link header rel="type" naming the
# Container type; the server assigns the new container's identifier.
# The header content is constant per operation, so it can safely live on
# the (per-rule, shared) conclusion blank node.
{
  ?op a lwst:CreateContainer ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:POST ;
      http:headers [
        a http:RequestHeader ;
        http:fieldName "Link" ;
        http:fieldValue '<https://www.w3.org/ns/lws#Container>; rel="type"'
      ] .
} .

# Create data resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#create-resource
# POST to the target container; the server creates a new data resource
# and assigns its identifier. The spec binds the initial content as the
# request body with an appropriate Content-Type header; content is
# per-step test data, so body and Content-Type are not derived here
# until the suite models it (cf. lwst:content).
{
  ?op a lwst:CreateDataResource ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:POST .
} .

# Update resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#update-resource
# PUT (full replacement) binding. PATCH is an alternative binding for
# partial updates, left out until the LWS spec settles update semantics.
# As with CreateDataResource, the replacement content is per-step test
# data, so body and Content-Type are not derived here.
{
  ?op a lwst:UpdateResource ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:PUT .
} .

# Delete resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#delete-resource
{
  ?op a lwst:DeleteResource ;
      lwst:target ?target .
  ?target lwst:value ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri ;
      http:mthd methods:DELETE .
} .

# ---- generic HTTP-in-RDF response values -> step parameters ----
#
# PROPOSAL -- assumed per-step RDF shape (to be reconciled with the
# final suite framing); each step being its own graph keeps the
# constant parameter IRIs scoped to that step:
#
#   ?op    http:resp ?resp .                        # request -> response
#   ?resp  a http:Response ;
#          http:sc statuses:Created ;
#          http:headers [ a http:ResponseHeader ;
#                         http:fieldName "Location" ;
#                         http:fieldValue ?location ] ;
#          http:body ?body .
#
#   ?step  lwst:operation  ?op ;
#          lwst:assertions ?assertion ;
#          lwst:bindings   ?binding .
#   ?assertion lwst:received param:status-code ;
#              lwst:expected statuses:OK .
#   ?binding   lwst:id       param:location ;
#              lwst:return   param:new-resource .
#
# The suite names generic parts of an HTTP response by well-known
# parameters. A parameter is an IRI denoting a variable, bound to its
# value with lwst:value:
#
#   param:status-code   lwst:value ?code .
#   param:location      lwst:value ?location .
#   param:content       lwst:value ?body .
#
# Each rule below resolves one well-known parameter against the actual
# http:Response (per step, i.e. within the step's own graph, so the
# constant parameter IRIs do not collide across steps).

# status code IRI -> param:status-code (http:sc ranges over
# http:StatusCode, i.e. the IRI in the http-status-codes namespace;
# http:statusCodeValue would instead give the numeric literal)
{
  ?resp a http:Response ;
        http:sc ?code .
}
=>
{
  param:status-code lwst:value ?code .
} .

# Location response header -> param:location
{
  ?resp a http:Response ;
        http:headers ?header .
  ?header http:fieldName "Location" ;
          http:fieldValue ?location .
}
=>
{
  param:location lwst:value ?location .
} .

# response body -> param:content
{
  ?resp a http:Response ;
        http:body ?body .
}
=>
{
  param:content lwst:value ?body .
} .

# step binding: copy the value of the parameter named by lwst:id to the
# parameter named by lwst:return
{
  ?binding lwst:id ?source ;
           lwst:return ?target .
  ?source lwst:value ?value .
}
=>
{
  ?target lwst:value ?value .
} .

# ---- ReadStorageDescription response content ----
#
# The response body is the storage description as RDF, modelled with
# Content-in-RDF: the message body is a cnt:ContentAsRDF whose
# cnt:graph is the description graph (a quoted graph / named graph).
#
#   cnt:ContentAsRDF rdfs:subClassOf cnt:Content .
#   cnt:graph rdfs:domain cnt:ContentAsRDF ; rdfs:range rdf:Resource .
#
#   ?resp a http:Response ;
#         http:sc statuses:OK ;
#         http:body [ a cnt:ContentAsRDF ;
#                      cnt:graph {
#                        <https://storage.example/>
#                            a lws:Storage ;
#                            did:service [ a lws:StorageRoot ;
#                                          did:serviceEndpoint
#                                            <https://storage.example/root/> ] .
#                      } ] .
#
# (cnt:ContentAsRDF / cnt:graph are not in the published Content-in-RDF
# 1.0 vocabulary yet -- deriving them here as proposed terms.)
#
# The full-N3 form reads the service out of the quoted graph (needs a
# reasoner with log:includes, e.g. eye-js):
#
#   { ?resp a http:Response ; http:body ?body .
#     ?body a cnt:ContentAsRDF ; cnt:graph ?g .
#     ?g log:includes { ?service a lws:StorageRoot ;
#                       did:serviceEndpoint ?storage-root } }
#   => { param:storage-root lwst:value ?storage-root . } .
#
# The n3.js reasoner cannot match nested graphs or log:includes, and
# cannot join premises across graphs (a premise spanning the default
# graph and a quoted graph does not fire). Its plain patterns do however
# match triples inside a quoted graph, so the description graph's
# StorageRoot service is read out directly. The step's own graph scopes
# this to the ReadStorageDescription step that produced the body.
{
  ?service a lws:StorageRoot ;
           did:serviceEndpoint ?storage-root .
}
=>
{
  param:storage-root lwst:value ?storage-root .
} .